  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1049 PM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge that brought record/near record temperatures\nto the forecast area the last two days will remain over the region\nthrough Thursday. This means a continuation of unseasonably warm\nweather, although the potential of record temperatures will drop\nslightly Wednesday as we see just a slight push of easterly winds\nacross the plains. That will result in just a couple degrees of \ncooling, with lower to mid 80s common for highs across the plains \nboth Wednesday and Thursday. Given a little moisture hanging under\nthe ridge, expect a similar convective scenario to Tuesday with \nisolated to scattered light showers/isolated storms mainly over \nthe high country. \n\nOn Friday, we'll start to get into west/southwest flow aloft as\nthe ridge starts to break down as an upper level trough moves into\nthe Pacific Northwest (PNW). This will support more downslope \nflow and a continuation of very warm temperatures. In fact, this \ncould get us close (30-40% chance) of tying another record high on\neither Thursday or Friday (Records are 85F and 87F, respectively).\nWith the increasing downslope, any isolated showers or storms \nwould be confined to the mountains. \n\nThere is excellent agreement that the warmth will persist for one\nmore day Saturday, before a pattern shift occurs. By Sunday, the\nPNW trough is still forecast to drop southward across California,\npicking up remnants of Hurricane Rachel. The trough has trended\ndeeper and farther west, which means the bulk of the precipitation\n(some heavy) will once again end up falling over the Desert \nSouthwest into Utah and perhaps western Colorado. For the most \npart, our forecast area will likely remain sheltered from any \nsignificant precipitation. There are only a couple outliers with \nheavier rain and mountain snow for us, and this might be because \nof the handling of yet the next tropical storm which is still in \nthe very early stages of expected development. Thus, the best \nchance of precipitation is now likely to hold off until Wednesday \nor Thursday but a lot of uncertainty exists there. The uncertainty\nsurrounding temperatures also holds true, with some ensemble \nmembers having a backdoor cool front reaching us Sunday or more \nlikely Monday. But given the farther west location of the upper \ntrough, it seems like the main push of cooler air would likely \nget delayed. We'll stick near the ensemble averages at this point \nbut should be worth noting that Monday's highs could range \nanywhere from the mid 40s to the mid 80s, with 25-75th percentiles\nshowing 60s-70s. A lot of spread but more confidence of cooling \nto autum-like temperatures toward the middle of next week.",
